  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures \nand lower humidity Sunday into early next week.\n\nBroad high pressure over the Great Lakes will track over the \nMid-Atlantic through Monday. This will bring a change in \nairmass with notably lower dew points. Highs in the low 80s for \nmost today and Monday. Overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to transition south of the Mid- \nAtlantic on Tuesday as upper level ridging builds over the\nsoutheast CONUS. Temperatures likely begin to creep up again \nduring this time, accompanied by increasing humidity as mid-\nlevel dry air tracks south of the region with more humid air \ncoming in from the north.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid week.\n\nUpper-level ridging over the Appalachians starts to flatten into \nzonal flow beginning Wednesday. As this occurs, a trough forming \nover Quebec will begin to track east across the northeast CONUS, \nwith a stemming cold front moving across the East Coast by Thursday. \nTemperatures gradually increase during this time, along with \nincreasing humidity ahead of the front. By Thursday, temps \npotentially reach the upper 80s to low 90s. \n\nAs this frontal boundary tracks through the Mid-Atlantic, showers\n& thunderstorms are possible across the region, beginning Wednesday\nevening and into Thursday. The 12z guidance remains unclear in \nterms of timing and the exact evolution of this system, so a lot\nof uncertainties remain. There is general agreement in trough\ndigging south into the region, but the exact strength and \ntiming of that trough is still being ironed out. Temperatures \nThursday are expected to be in the 80s to near 90, with dew \npoint in the 60s to perhaps low 70s. This should yield plenty of\nCAPE south of the front itself and allow for at least some \nthunderstorm development if the forcing arrives near peak \nheating. The severe threat is still in question, but remains \nnon-zero. If the trough tracks far enough south, there could be \na fair amount of wind shear to work with as well, which would \nincrease that severe threat. Some of the AI guidance \n(particularly the WxNext) do highlight our region, so it is \ncertainly something to keep a close eye on. Will continue to \nmonitor latest guidance in hopes of honing in on a more \nconfident solution. \n\nAs this cold front transitions offshore towards the end of next \nweek, temps drop again & conditions become much drier heading into \nthe weekend as high pressure moves in overhead.",
